---
name: agent-relay
description: Run long or failure-prone Codex work as durable, bounded subagent phases with machine-readable checkpoints, exact Git/test ledgers, immutable-SHA review, and fresh-context recovery. Use when coordinating subagents, when context-window exhaustion is a risk, when an agent errored or disappeared, or when work must survive across Codex sessions.
---

# Agent Relay

Use `~/.codex/agent-relay/bin/` as the control plane. Treat `state.json` and checkpoint JSON as authoritative; a worker message is never proof of completion.

## Start a relay

Require a clean Git worktree, then run:

```bash
~/.codex/agent-relay/bin/relay-init \
  --repo /absolute/repo \
  --objective "bounded objective" \
  --implementation-slices 1 \
  --max-elapsed 1800 \
  --max-tools 40 \
  --checkpoint-seconds 600 \
  --checkpoint-tools 10
```

Save the returned launch envelope. Spawn exactly its `agent_type`, `worktree`, and prompt with `fork_turns: "none"`. Never use an inherited-context fork for relay workers.

## Coordinator protocol

1. Assign one worker to one phase only: discovery, each implementation slice, validation, review, then publish.
2. Require the worker to call `relay-update --start --worker-id` before other work.
3. Count every non-relay tool call with `relay-update --tool-calls 1`.
4. Run `relay-monitor --run ID` periodically and before waiting. Exit `2` means checkpoint now; exit `3` means stop, checkpoint as errored, and relaunch.
5. Accept completion only after `relay-validate --run ID --strict` passes and state marks the phase completed.
6. Obtain the next fresh launch envelope from `relay-next-handoff --run ID`.
7. Give reviewers only the recorded `immutable_sha`. Never ask them to review a moving branch or dirty worktree.
8. Keep publish as a separate phase and perform only explicitly authorized publication.

### Status language

When reporting relay progress, name only the highest rung proven by the durable ledger:

1. specified;
2. planned;
3. implemented locally;
4. committed at an immutable SHA;
5. focused validation passed;
6. full validation passed;
7. independent review clean;
8. published or deployed;
9. verified live;
10. accepted or adopted.

These states are not synonyms and cannot be skipped by implication. A commit does not prove tests. Passing focused tests does not erase a failing required suite. A clean review does not prove deployment. Deployment does not prove the user path works. User-visible proof does not prove adoption.

If a later gate is blocked, report the completed lower rungs and the exact next unproven transition. Substantial implementation progress does not make a convergence stall, dirty worktree, failed review, or missing publication receipt disappear.

## Worker protocol

Read the launch envelope, `state.json`, and `HANDOFF.md`. Work only the named phase.

```bash
relay=~/.codex/agent-relay/bin
"$relay/relay-update" --run RUN_ID --start --worker-id AGENT_ID
"$relay/relay-update" --run RUN_ID --tool-calls 1
"$relay/relay-monitor" --run RUN_ID
"$relay/relay-update" --run RUN_ID --checkpoint --summary-file /path/to/phase-summary.md --note "checkpoint"
```

Checkpoint before either cadence limit, and immediately before risky or context-heavy work. Implementation phases must commit their bounded changes. Validation must record every exact command and exit code:

```bash
"$relay/relay-update" --run RUN_ID --test "npm test -- --runInBand" --test-exit 0 --test-summary "all suites passed"
```

Finish only through the ledger:

```bash
"$relay/relay-update" --run RUN_ID --complete --summary-file /path/to/phase-summary.md --note "phase result"
```

Completion atomically writes a final checkpoint and fails if the worktree is dirty, the budget is exhausted, validation lacks passing current-SHA tests, or review moved off its immutable SHA.

## Recover an errored or missing worker

First interrupt any still-live worker. Do not reuse its thread. If it recorded an error, call `relay-next-handoff --run ID`; if it vanished while marked in progress, call:

```bash
~/.codex/agent-relay/bin/relay-next-handoff --run ID --recover --note "worker lost/interrupted"
```

This captures the live Git fingerprint, marks the attempt errored, and emits a replacement envelope for the same phase. Spawn the replacement with `fork_turns: "none"`; it reconstructs context only from the durable ledger and handoff.

Read [references/state-contract.md](references/state-contract.md) when changing budgets, diagnosing a refused transition, or auditing a run.

