Focused Python and TypeScript tests pass, but several untested failure and integration paths can lose escalations, misrepresent dry-run state, or break existing task synchronization. These correctness issues should be fixed before merging.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Retry escalations unless delivery actually succeeded — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/scripts/brain/client_state_gmail.py:502-505
  When `send-telegram` fails for a message containing both fileable and ambiguous resolutions, the outer exception handler persists a partial row while leaving the ambiguous resolution as `outcome="escalated"`. On retry, `Ledger.escalated_for` interprets that row as proof of delivery and skips `_send_escalation`, permanently losing the alert. Record escalation completion only after a successful send.

- [P1] Mark budget-exceeded dry runs as simulated — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/scripts/brain/client_state_gmail.py:307-312
  When a dry run exceeds `--max-usd`, this row leaves `simulated` at its false default. The ledger and digest therefore describe a paid dry-run extraction as real, and any ambiguous resolution in the row can suppress the later live escalation. Set the row's simulation state from `cfg.dry_run`.

- [P1] Distinguish claim failures from lock contention — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/scripts/brain/client_state_gmail.py:541-542
  If the claim command exits nonzero because the claims directory is unwritable or the CLI encounters another operational error, `acquire` collapses that result to `None` and this path records a misleading lock-held refusal while leaving the success receipt untouched. Exceptions such as a missing or timed-out executable also escape because acquisition occurs outside the guarded `try`; only an actual `already-claimed` response should produce exit 2, while transport failures should produce the structured exit-3 failure path.

- [P1] Preserve Multica's explicit human claim path — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/src/bus/task.ts:1173-1178
  When Multica assigns a pending human-exempt task to Josh, `pollMulticaIssues` calls `claimTask(paths, taskId, "human")` without options at `src/bus/multica/poll.ts:175`. This new guard rejects that existing synchronization path even though the claimant is the human, so the poll records an error and leaves the task pending. Allow the human claimant or update that deliberate writeback caller to pass the override.

- [P2] Include task type when computing default due dates — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/src/bus/task.ts:730-730
  For direct `createTask(..., { type: "human" })` calls whose assignee, project, and title do not otherwise imply human class, `taskType` is persisted only after the provisional `classifyTask` call, which omits it. A normal-priority human task therefore receives the build default of seven days instead of the human-class two-day cap despite later classifying as human.

- [P2] Isolate the Gmail digest from secrets-file failures — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/scripts/brain/meeting_loop_watch.py:191-191
  If `orgs/clearworksai/secrets.env` is missing or unreadable, `parse_env_file` raises before either independently guarded section runs. This prevents the Gmail digest from running even though only the Fireflies section needs that file, defeating the new section-independence behavior.

- [P2] Search ledger history before invoking the model again — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/scripts/brain/extract_email.py:360-362
  The cache lookup examines only the latest observation row. If a binding set cycles from `A` to `A+B` and back to `A`, or message content reverts to an earlier digest, an extraction with the exact same identity already exists in older ledger history but Claude is invoked again, violating the stated at-most-one-call guarantee for `(source_ref, digest, slugs)`.

- [P2] Persist paid dry-run extraction after downstream errors — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/scripts/brain/client_state_gmail.py:341-342
  If a dry run successfully pays for extraction and then page locking, reading, or preview generation raises, `_persist_partial` returns solely because `cfg.dry_run` is true. No ledger row or extraction cache is retained, so retrying the same dry run invokes Claude again despite the cache and one-call guarantees.

- [P2] Identify invariant pages by relative path — /Users/joshweiss/code/cortextos-worktrees/client-state-gmail-v1/scripts/brain/client_state_digest.py:82-84
  If distinct pages in different folders share a stem—for example `clients/acme.md` and `orgs/acme.md`—the invariant collector records both as `acme` and deduplicates them. A domain or CRM organization name declared on both pages is therefore reported as belonging to only one page, so the required duplicate-page invariant never fires.
